
====================================================================================================
S1. The kernel's FREQUENCY channel cannot source theta -- unimodular on the live branch
====================================================================================================
  K(-w^2+i0) = (sqrt(4w^2-1) + i)/(2w)      [retarded branch]
    Re K = sqrt(4w^2-1)/(2w) = sqrt(1 - 1/(4w^2)),   Im K = 1/(2w)
    |K|^2 = (W^2+1)/(4w^2) with W^2 = 4w^2-1  ->  1   (sympy-exact)
  [OK] the kernel is EXACTLY unimodular on the oscillatory branch
  [OK] K = exp[i*arcsin(a0/2c omega)] -- a PURE PHASE LAG, zero amplitude modification
  So an amplitude-channel theta(y) would need |K| to vary with frequency. It cannot.
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z  : branch point omega_b = a0/2c = 1.561e-19 1/s -> period  1275.4 Gyr
    alt rho_total cH0             : branch point omega_b = a0/2c = 1.885e-19 1/s -> period  1056.5 Gyr
  Every bound orbit therefore sits at w >> 1/2, deep inside the unimodular regime. The
  only frequency-dependent amplitude effect is second order in the phase lag:
  carrier                     omega (1/s)  w=omega c/a0   phi (rad)   1-cos phi
  cluster member orbit          3.241e-17     1.038e+02   4.817e-03   1.160e-05
  LSB dwarf internal            4.321e-16     1.384e+03   3.613e-04   6.526e-08
  [OK] frequency channel bounds any theta y-dependence at <= 1.2e-05, ~4-5 orders below the banked 6-13% spread

====================================================================================================
S2. Theorem B forces QUADRATURE addition, not Milgrom's linear Eq-34 ansatz
====================================================================================================
  KERNEL_THEORY.md Theorem B (exact for any timelike worldline, curved space included):
      u_mu Box_u u^mu = -|a|^2   =>   <Box_u>_u = +|a|^2   exactly
  so the inertia argument is built from |a|^2, and the total field enters as
      |a_in + a_ex|^2 = a_in^2 + a_ex^2 + 2 a_in.a_ex        (QUADRATURE + cross term)
  Milgrom's EFE ansatz (arXiv:2208.07073v3 Eq 34) instead posits
      A = a_in + a_ex * theta(omega_ex/omega_in)              (LINEAR in a_ex)
  They differ even at theta == 1. Size of that structural gap at the frozen fiducial:
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   linear x= 2.30 boost= 1.241 | quadrature x= 2.02 boost= 1.277 | sigma differs  +1.46%
    alt rho_total cH0              linear x= 1.91 boost= 1.296 | quadrature x= 1.68 boost= 1.342 | sigma differs  +1.75%
  [OK] quadrature gives a strictly SMALLER argument than linear addition
  This is the load-bearing difference. The LINEAR form is what lets theta multiply a_ex
  and swing the argument by ~3x across infall phase. Under quadrature nothing multiplies
  a_ex at all, and the y-dependence is forced into the cross term -- computed next.

====================================================================================================
S3. The cross-term coherence, DERIVED with no free input
====================================================================================================
  a_in oscillates at omega_in, a_ex varies at omega_ex (the member's cluster orbit); their
  dot product beats at omega_in -/+ omega_ex. Averaged over the closure window T_w the
  difference beat carries the standard coherence factor
      C(y) = sinc[ Q (1-y)/2 ],   Q = omega_in * T_w,   y = omega_ex/omega_in
  T_w is supplied by the framework itself: the kernel memory tau_mem = 2c/a0, capped by
  the age of the universe. AND Q NEEDS NO SEPARATE INPUT -- since omega_in = omega_ex/y,
      Q(y) = (omega_ex * T_w) / y,   so C depends only on the DIMENSIONLESS  P = omega_ex*T_w
      C(y) = sinc[ P (1-y) / (2y) ]      (P = cluster orbits per memory window, x 2pi)
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   tau_mem=  203.0 Gyr, T_w= 13.80 Gyr (age-capped)  ->  P =  14.11
    alt rho_total cH0              tau_mem=  168.1 Gyr, T_w= 13.80 Gyr (age-capped)  ->  P =  14.11
  [OK] tau_mem exceeds the age of the universe on both footings, so T_w = t_age

  Coherence across infall phase (canonical, P = 14.11):
       y      Q(y)      C(y)
    0.05     282.2    0.0064
    0.10     141.1    0.0097
    0.25      56.4    0.0347
    0.50      28.2    0.0989
    0.80      17.6    0.5564
    1.00      14.1    1.0000
    1.20      11.8    0.7850
    1.50       9.4    0.3020
  C is NOT the monotone-decreasing theta Milgrom posits: it is small at low y, PEAKS AT
  y = 1, and falls again above it. The peak is where the internal and orbital frequencies
  lock, so the cross term stops averaging away. That is a qualitatively different shape.
  [OK] coherence peaks at y=1 rather than at y->0 (opposite of theta's shape)

====================================================================================================
S4. The y range real LSB cluster carriers actually span -- computed
====================================================================================================
  y = omega_ex/omega_in, omega_ex = v_cl/R_cl (orbit about cluster), omega_in = sigma_in/R_e.
  Carriers per frozen spec E1: mu_0,r fainter than 24 mag/arcsec^2, R_e >= 1 kpc.
  y spans 0.0117 to 1.5000 across that carrier x cluster grid
  frozen spec assumes y in [0.05, 1.5]
  [OK] real carriers DO span the spec's phase range (it reaches y~1.5 for the most diffuse carriers at small cluster radii, and extends BELOW the spec floor) -- the phase lever is real, so the spread question is settled by amplitude, not by range

====================================================================================================
S5. The sigma-spread re-derived under the framework's own closure, both footings
====================================================================================================
  (i) BANKED route -- Milgrom's LINEAR ansatz with the three postulated theta forms over
      the spec's four y values, reproduced as the like-for-like baseline.
      canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   spread   6.2% -  11.8%
      alt rho_total cH0              spread   7.5% -  14.1%
      both-footing band 6.2% - 14.1%   (GAP_STATEMENT: 6.2-14.1%)
  [OK] the banked band reproduces from its own inputs

  (ii) DERIVED route, max-min convention (same convention as the banked band above),
       evaluated on the spec's y range so the comparison is not a range artifact.
      canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   x in [1.9569,2.3000] -> spread 1.855%
      alt rho_total cH0              x in [1.6209,1.9051] -> spread 2.213%

  (iii) DERIVED route, POPULATION-RMS convention -- what the frozen estimator actually
        measures (E4: excess VARIANCE of FJ residuals). Monte Carlo over the carrier
        population; two priors on y, reported both ways, neither tuned.
      log-uniform in y   canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   RMS spread = 0.4127%
      log-uniform in y   alt rho_total cH0              RMS spread = 0.4936%
      uniform in y       canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   RMS spread = 0.6052%
      uniform in y       alt rho_total cH0              RMS spread = 0.7204%

  DERIVED spread: 2.213% (max-min)   0.7204% (population RMS, worst prior)
  BANKED  band  : 6.2% - 14.1%
  Suppression factor vs banked low end: 2.8x (max-min), 9x (RMS)
  [OK] derived max-min spread (2.21%) is below the banked band's low end (6.2%)
  [OK] the population-RMS spread is smaller than max-min, as it must be -- the coherent enhancement lives near y=1, not across the whole population

  (iv) ROBUSTNESS TO THE WINDOW SHAPE -- honesty check on my own modelling choice.
       The sinc above comes from a BOXCAR average over T_w. That is MY choice, not the
       framework's: the kernel's memory carries a Herglotz weight, and I capped it at
       t_age by hand. So re-do it with an EXPONENTIAL memory (Lorentzian coherence,
       C = 1/(1+(P(1-y)/y)^2)) and with a Gaussian window, and see what actually moves.
       window                         max-min   pop-RMS  peak y
       boxcar  -> sinc                 1.855%   0.4120%   1.000
       exponential -> Lorentzian       1.446%   0.2206%   1.000
       Gaussian -> Gaussian            1.449%   0.3234%   1.000
  [OK] EVERY window shape gives a max-min spread (1.45-1.86%) below the banked low end (6.2%) -- the suppression is not a boxcar artifact
  [OK] window choice moves the amplitude by only 1.28x, so the ORDER of the result is robust even though the exact shape is not derived
       WHAT IS ROBUST: quadrature-vs-linear (Theorem B, exact); the cross term being the
       only y-channel (algebraic); suppression ~1/Q for incommensurate frequencies and a
       peak at commensurability (generic for ANY averaging window). WHAT IS NOT DERIVED:
       the precise line shape, and hence the exact peak width. Quoted accordingly.

====================================================================================================
S6. Consequences -- carrier count, and the SHAPE the frozen estimator is not built for
====================================================================================================
  Estimator (frozen, GAP_STATEMENT S3): z ~ (s p)^2 / (eps_eff^2 sqrt(2/N))
  => N(3sigma) = 2 (3 eps_eff^2 / (s p)^2)^2,  so N scales as s^-4.
                spread s  eps_meas  eps_FJ    N(3 sigma)
               banked hi 14.067%      0.25    0.15     2.563e+03
               banked hi 14.067%      0.10    0.10     1.419e+02
               banked lo  6.228%      0.25    0.15     6.670e+04
               banked lo  6.228%      0.10    0.10     3.693e+03
         DERIVED max-min  2.213%      0.25    0.15     4.184e+06
         DERIVED max-min  2.213%      0.10    0.10     2.316e+05
         DERIVED pop-RMS  0.720%      0.25    0.15     3.726e+08
         DERIVED pop-RMS  0.720%      0.10    0.10     2.063e+07

  At the ELT tier (eps_meas=eps_FJ=0.10) the carrier requirement inflates by
    6.272e+01x on the max-min reading   -> N ~ 2.32e+05
    5.586e+03x on the pop-RMS reading   -> N ~ 2.06e+07
  against ~3693 carriers on the banked band. For scale, 4MOST-CHANCES
  delivers ~300k spectra total across 150 clusters, and only a small LSB subset of those
  would ever have resolved internal dispersions at the 10% tier.
  [OK] on the estimator's own (RMS) convention the requirement exceeds the FULL CHANCES spectrum budget (~3e5) even before the LSB/resolved-sigma cut

  SHAPE FINDING (independent of the amplitude, and arguably the more useful half).
  The frozen E4 SIGN statistic contrasts first-infall against settled members and expects
  a MONOTONE trend in infall phase. The derived coherence is NOT monotone: it peaks at
  y = 1 and declines on both sides. A monotone two-class contrast integrates across that
  peak and partially cancels. Quantified -- two-class difference vs the full range:
    canonical rho_DE cH_Lambda/Z   two-class |diff| = 0.7717%  vs max-min 1.855%
    alt rho_total cH0              two-class |diff| = 0.9203%  vs max-min 2.213%
  So the pre-registered sign statistic is testing for a shape the framework's own closure
  does not produce. A y-RESOLVED statistic (bin carriers by omega_ex/omega_in and look for
  a peak at 1) is strictly better matched, and is free to adopt -- it needs no new data,
  only a different binning of the same carriers.

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. theta need not be borrowed, and the kernel cannot supply it: |K| = 1 exactly on the
     oscillatory branch, so the frequency channel offers <= 1e-05 of amplitude change.
  2. Theorem B forces the external field in through |a|^2 -- QUADRATURE. Milgrom's Eq 34
     adds it linearly with a multiplying theta. Under this framework's own closure there
     is no slot for a multiplying phase function at all.
  3. The surviving y-dependence is the cross-term coherence C(y) = sinc[P(1-y)/2y] with
     P = omega_ex*t_age ~ 14.1, and it needs NO free input.
  4. Re-derived spread: 2.21% (max-min) / 0.720% (population RMS) against the
     banked 6.2-14.1%. Suppression 3x to 9x.
  5. Carrier requirement at the ELT tier: ~2.1e+07 rather than ~3693.
     The GAP_STATEMENT reopen conditions (ELT ~2032, or an FJ floor <= 0.15) were priced
     against a spread the framework's own closure does not deliver, so the 2032 window is
     NOT the real gate. This narrows on THEORY grounds, before any data argument.
  6. The frozen E4 sign statistic tests a monotone trend; the derived signal peaks at y=1.
     Re-binning by omega_ex/omega_in is a free improvement to the pre-registration.

  WHAT IS *NOT* CLAIMED. This does not say the channel is impossible in principle -- the
  MG contrast is untouched (MG remains exactly zero, the framework remains nonzero, so the
  signature is still MG-impossible in principle). Nor is it 'unreachable at any tier': the
  requirement is large but finite. An earlier draft of this script asserted it exceeded the
  number of galaxies in the observable universe; that was wrong by many orders and its own
  check caught it. The defensible statement is narrower: at the derived amplitude the
  observable is out of reach of every survey now on the books, including the ELT programme
  the gap statement was written to justify.

  WHAT WOULD OVERTURN THIS (falsifiable, not defended):
   (a) A different closure. Theorem B fixes the first MOMENT exactly, but KERNEL_THEORY.md
       Finding C records the off-circular TIME-WEIGHTING as a free O(1) choice. A weighting
       that both reproduces the RAR and restores a LINEAR a_ex channel would bring the
       banked band back. None is known, and the RAR constraint is severe.
   (b) Carriers piled up near y = 1. The coherence peak is real; a population concentrated
       there gets the full cross term. That is a checkable claim about cluster orbital
       structure, not a free parameter -- and it is what the re-binned statistic tests.
   (c) A much longer effective averaging window than t_age, which would raise P and
       sharpen the peak; or a much shorter one, which would broaden it toward full
       coherence. T_w = min(2c/a0, t_age) is the framework's own choice, not a knob.

  PROVENANCE. Milgrom's theta is itself a modified-inertia construction and was the right
  thing to try first; this is not a criticism of it. The finding is that THIS framework's
  completion makes a stricter choice than Eq 34, and the corpus had been quoting Eq 34's
  consequences as its own.

  ONE CITATION CAVEAT, STATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. The LINEAR form 'A = a_in +
  a_ex*theta(y)' is taken from how this repo's own files record Milgrom 2022 Eq 34
  (prep_2026/sigma_spread/rederive_spread_and_power.py and GAP_STATEMENT.md S1). The
  primary source was NOT re-read while producing this script. If Eq 34 is in fact a vector
  or quadrature statement, then S2's structural gap narrows or vanishes -- but the load-
  bearing result does not depend on it: S1 (no amplitude in the frequency channel), S3
  (the cross term is the only y-channel, suppressed ~1/Q and peaked at commensurability),
  and S5 (the repriced spread) are all internal to THIS framework's closure and stand on
  Theorem B alone. Verifying Eq 34 against the primary source is a cheap open item.
====================================================================================================
